---
// Privacy. Every claim here is traceable to code, and the page says plainly what it cannot claim:
// analytics does load for real visitors. The footer has always asserted "Private" as a tooltip
// with nothing behind it; this is the thing behind it.
//
// If you change the analytics guard, the storage keys, the service worker or the feedback
// delivery path, change this page in the same commit.
import BaseLayout from '@layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { generatePageTitle } from '@lib/titles';
import { withBase } from '@lib/paths';
import { GA_MEASUREMENT_ID } from '@lib/analytics/guard';
import { PRIVACY_LINE } from '@lib/privacy';

const description =
  `${PRIVACY_LINE} No account, no database, no server sees your input. Analytics is the one exception, named below.`;

const updated = '6 October 2026';
---

<BaseLayout title={generatePageTitle('privacy')} description={description} maxWidth="tool">
  <article class="page-doc">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="lede">{description}</p>
    <p class="updated">Last updated {updated}</p>

    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No tool sends what you type anywhere. There is nothing on the other end to send it to.</li>
      <li>There is no account, no login, and no database. Nothing is stored against your identity because there is no identity to store it against.</li>
      <li>Anything a tool saves for you is saved on your device, and you can export or delete all of it from <a href={withBase('/settings/')}>Settings</a>.</li>
      <li>Google Analytics does load for ordinary visitors. That is the one thing on this site that reports back, and it is described below.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What runs in your browser</h2>
    <p>
      ToyTools is a static site. There is no application server. When you open a tool, the page and
      its code are downloaded and everything after that happens on your machine: the text you paste
      into a word counter, the numbers in a loan calculator, the token in a JWT decoder. No tool
      transmits any of it, because there is nothing on the other end to transmit it to.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is also why the tools keep working with no network once you have loaded them, and why
      they can be installed to your home screen and used offline.
    </p>
    <p>
      Kinetic Sand can read the phone's tilt so grains run downhill. That reading is never sent
      anywhere.
    </p>

    <h2>What is stored on your device</h2>
    <p>
      Some tools remember things between visits, for example a notepad's contents, a tracker's log,
      or your preferred currency. That data is written to your browser's local storage under keys
      beginning <code>toytools:</code> and <code>toytools.</code>, and it stays on that device in
      that browser. It is never uploaded.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Per-tool state</strong> (<code>toytools:&lt;tool&gt;</code>) such as notes, todo items and tracker entries.</li>
      <li><strong>Tool settings</strong> on the other tools, such as a converter's direction, a calculator's units or a generator's options. Never what you typed into them.</li>
      <li><strong>Preferences</strong> (<code>toytools:prefs</code>) such as display currency, Feel sound/haptics/motion choices, and the theme (<code>toytools.theme</code>).</li>
      <li><strong>Favourites and recently used tools</strong>, to make the catalog navigable.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A tool whose notice reads "Nothing stored unless you choose to save it" keeps what you type
      on the page only: text, dates, body measurements, a QR code's content or Wi-Fi password.
      Reloading the page clears it, and nothing you type is written into the address bar on its
      own. A link holds your values only when you press Copy link, and opening such a link fills
      the form from it. A tool's Recent conversions list lives in the open tab and is gone on
      reload too. Older versions of the site saved some of that input, and a body profile for
      the health calculators, in this browser; it is removed the next time you open any page here.
    </p>
    <p>
      Clearing your browser's site data erases all of it permanently. Because some of it is not
      recoverable, such as months of tracker entries, there is an export on the
      <a href={withBase('/settings/')}>Settings</a> page that writes everything to a single JSON
      file you can keep or move to another device.
    </p>

    <h2>What is sent, and to whom</h2>
    <h3>Google Analytics</h3>
    <p>
      This site loads Google Analytics (measurement ID <code>{GA_MEASUREMENT_ID}</code>) to count
      visits and see which tools get used. It records the usual things a web analytics product
      records: pages viewed, approximate location derived from your IP address, device and browser
      type, and referrer. No tool passes what you type to it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some calculators keep their inputs in the page address: the bill-splitting and UPI
      calculators as you type, and any calculator you open from a shared link (see Links you share
      below). The site
      gives Google Analytics that address without the part after the question mark, for the page
      you are on and for the page you came from, so those inputs are left out. Google Analytics can
      also count a page view by itself when you return to a page with the Back button, and then it
      reads the full address from your browser. Site searches are read from the full address too.
      If any page address on this site has a <code>q</code>, <code>s</code>, <code>search</code>,
      <code>query</code> or <code>keyword</code> parameter after the question mark, Google
      Analytics records its value as a search term. That covers the words you type on the search
      page, and the "Suggest it" links, which open the feedback page at
      <code>/feedback/?type=new&amp;q=</code> followed by the words you typed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Analytics loads only on <code>toytoolsapp.com</code> (including
      <code>www.toytoolsapp.com</code>). It is deliberately not loaded on any other address the site
      is served from, inside a frame on another page, when the site is running locally, or under
      automated browser testing, so development and test traffic never reaches it. If you use a
      tracker blocker or browser-level blocking, nothing on the site depends on analytics loading
      and everything keeps working.
    </p>

    <h3>Feedback</h3>
    <p>
      The <a href={withBase('/feedback/')}>feedback page</a> does not submit anywhere. It composes a
      message and hands it to your own email client, so you can see exactly what is being sent
      before you send it, and you are the one sending it. There is no form endpoint, no relay and
      no database. If you tick the option to include what you typed into a tool, that text goes into
      the message you can read; on tools where the input could be a secret, such as the JWT decoder
      and the hash generators, the option is not offered at all.
    </p>

    <h3>Links you share</h3>
    <p>
      Calculators marked "Nothing stored unless you choose to save it", such as the compound
      interest, date difference and health tools, have a "Copy link" button you press deliberately.
      It builds a link holding the values you entered into that calculator, so you can bookmark a
      calculation or send it to someone, and opening that link fills the calculator in. Those tools
      never write your inputs into the page address on their own, so nothing you type into them
      ends up in your browser history or in a link by accident. If you open such a link and then
      change a value, the address goes back to the plain page, so a reload does not bring the
      link's values back.
    </p>
    <p>
      The bill-splitting and UPI calculators, which keep their data in this browser, keep their
      inputs in the page address as you type, so a link to them carries those numbers. Tools whose
      input may be a secret never put anything in the address at all, and offer no link action.
    </p>

    <h2>Cookies</h2>
    <p>
      ToyTools sets no cookies of its own. Google Analytics sets its own cookies to recognise repeat
      visits. Everything the site itself remembers uses local storage, not cookies.
    </p>

    <h2>Other services</h2>
    <p>
      There are none. No advertising, no comment system, no embedded videos, no chat widget, no
      social buttons, and no web fonts fetched from a third-party CDN (the fonts are served from
      this site). The service worker that makes tools work offline stores pages in your browser's
      own cache and talks to nothing else.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>
      ToyTools is a general-purpose set of utilities and is not directed at children. It asks for no
      personal information at any point.
    </p>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>
      Ask through the <a href={withBase('/feedback/')}>feedback page</a>. If something on this page
      does not match what the site actually does, that is a bug worth reporting.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-doc {
    padding: var(--space-10) 0 var(--space-12);
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }

  .updated {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--color-text-subtle);
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
  }

  .page-doc h2 {
    margin-top: var(--space-12);
  }

  .page-doc h3 {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
  }

  .page-doc p,
  .page-doc ul {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
    line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
  }

  .page-doc ul {
    list-style: disc;
    padding-left: var(--space-6);
  }

  .page-doc li {
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
  }

  .page-doc code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    background: var(--color-surface);
    padding: 1px 4px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
  }
</style>
